<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The privacy manifest for this pod's own binary (destiny NGH-784).

	  WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT ONLY IN THE CONSUMER APP. Apple evaluates
	  ITMS-91053 per Mach-O binary. `Frameworks/TensorFlowLiteC.framework/
	  TensorFlowLiteC` is a STATIC Mach-O object, not a dylib, so it does not
	  ship as a binary of its own: the linker folds it into the framework this
	  pod builds, `mediapipe_face_mesh.framework`, and that is the binary in
	  which `_stat` / `_fstat` show up undefined. Before this file the pod set
	  `vendored_frameworks` and no `resource_bundles`, so upstream TensorFlow's
	  own declaration at `src/include/tensorflow/lite/ios/
	  TensorFlowLiteC.xcprivacy` was carried in the repository and never
	  copied into any built product — every consumer had to re-declare it at
	  app level or be rejected at upload.

	  The declaration below is upstream TensorFlow's, unchanged: TFLite calls
	  `stat`/`fstat` to mmap the `.tflite` models the consumer bundles, and
	  C617.1 is "files inside the app or its container", which is what those
	  models are. 0A2A.1 (user-granted files), 3B52.1 and DDA9.1 do not apply.

	  The three empty keys are deliberate and true for this pod: face-mesh
	  inference is on-device, this pod opens no network connection, collects
	  nothing and tracks nobody. That is a different claim from the consumer
	  app's, which is why the app keeps its own manifest — a manifest speaks
	  only for the binary that carries it.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
